# ==============================================================================
# SWAY CONFIGURATION - WAYLAND MINIMALIST ARCHITECTURE
# ==============================================================================

### Variables
#
# Logo key. Use Mod1 for Alt.
set $mod Mod4

# Home row direction keys, like vim
set $left h
set $down j
set $up k
set $right l

# Preferred Applications
set $term kitty

# application opener
# --columns is only set here, not in the shared wofi/config -- every
# other wofi-backed tool in this desktop (wifi/bluetooth pickers,
# calculator, keybind search, emoji picker, cliphist) is a plain
# single-column list, and a grid would break their readability. Width
# comes from wofi/config's own width=60%, shared by every wofi popup in
# this desktop, not repeated here.
#
# --show drun,run (both modes combined) used to be here, matching what
# this line had before any of this -- dropped the ",run" deliberately
# after finding a real sway parsing gotcha, not as an unrelated
# simplification: sway's own command syntax uses a bare comma to chain
# multiple commands on one bindsym line (`bindsym x cmd1, cmd2`), and
# `exec`'s argument isn't exempt from that -- a literal comma anywhere in
# the string handed to `exec`, even one meant for the program being
# launched, gets treated as a sway command separator. `wofi --show
# drun,run --columns 4` was silently getting truncated to just `wofi
# --show drun` the moment `exec` ran it -- confirmed directly by
# comparing the actual running process's real command line
# (`ps -o cmd -p <pid>`) against what was configured, after a real
# ydotool-simulated keypress (not just a manual shell reproduction of
# the command, which doesn't go through sway's exec parsing at all and
# had been silently passing every earlier verification of this). This is
# exactly why the grid kept reporting as "still one column" despite every
# earlier check looking fine -- --columns 4 was never actually reaching
# wofi in the first place. "run" mode (launch an arbitrary $PATH
# executable by typed name, not from the app database) wasn't part of
# what was actually asked for ("application search") anyway, so it's
# left out rather than chasing a quoting/escaping workaround to keep it.
set $menu wofi --show drun --columns 4

# calculator
set $calc ~/.local/bin/wofi-calc.sh

# Every popup keybinding below routes through toggle-popup.sh -- asked
# directly for these to toggle (press the same keybinding again to close
# it, not just always open a new one) rather than being fire-and-open
# only. Escape already closed the wofi-based ones on its own (wofi's own
# native behavior, nothing this repo had to add) -- toggle-popup.sh adds
# the other half: a marker file per keybinding tracks whether *that*
# popup is the one currently open, so the right process gets signaled
# closed instead of just guessing "any wofi". See the script's own
# comments for the full reasoning.

# clipboard history
bindsym $mod+m exec ~/.local/bin/toggle-popup.sh clipboard wofi sh -c "cliphist list | wofi --dmenu | cliphist decode | wl-copy"

# Launch Wofi Emoji Picker
bindsym $mod+period exec ~/.local/bin/toggle-popup.sh emoji wofi wofi-emoji

# Previously only reachable by clicking waybar's network module, no
# keyboard path existed at all
# Wi-Fi picker: scan, connect, toggle
bindsym $mod+Shift+w exec ~/.local/bin/toggle-popup.sh wifi wofi ~/.local/bin/wifi-picker.py

# Previously waybar-click-only, same gap as Wi-Fi above
# Bluetooth picker: pair, connect, toggle
bindsym $mod+Shift+b exec ~/.local/bin/toggle-popup.sh bluetooth wofi ~/.local/bin/bluetooth-picker.py

# Previously waybar-click-only, same gap as Wi-Fi/Bluetooth above
# Docker picker: view running containers, restart, stop
bindsym $mod+Shift+d exec ~/.local/bin/toggle-popup.sh docker wofi ~/.local/bin/docker-picker.py

# Matches the same keyboard-path convention as every other waybar-click
# picker above -- notification bell was waybar-click-only otherwise
# Notification history: view past notifications, sorted newest first
bindsym $mod+Shift+n exec ~/.local/bin/toggle-popup.sh notifhistory wofi ~/.local/bin/notification-history.py

# Same gap as every picker above -- the power menu (nwg-bar) was only
# ever reachable by clicking waybar's custom/power module, no keyboard
# path. Tab/Shift+Tab already move focus between its buttons on their
# own -- nwg-bar requests exclusive layer-shell keyboard focus itself
# (LAYER_SHELL_KEYBOARD_MODE_EXCLUSIVE, confirmed in its own source),
# and real GtkButtons in a GtkBox get Tab-chain navigation from GTK for
# free on top of that -- nothing this repo had to wire up. Verified
# safely without ever actually pressing Enter (every one of these five
# actions is disruptive/hard to reverse -- lock, logout, suspend,
# reboot, shutdown -- not something to trigger just to test a
# keybinding): queried nwg-bar's own AT-SPI accessibility tree directly
# (read-only, can't activate anything) after each simulated Tab/
# Shift+Tab and read back which button actually reports itself focused.
# Confirmed the full cycle both directions with wraparound: Lock ->
# Logout -> Suspend -> Reboot -> Shutdown -> (wraps) -> Lock forward,
# and Lock -> (wraps) -> Shutdown going back with Shift+Tab. No visible
# focus-ring styling on top of this yet -- works correctly, just not
# obvious to the eye which button Tab landed on.
bindsym $mod+Shift+p exec ~/.local/bin/toggle-popup.sh powermenu nwg-bar nwg-bar -i 40

# rmpc -- terminal music player, replacing termusic entirely (removed:
# its own search was the only thing ever broken, but the whole tool got
# swapped out rather than kept around as a half-used shell -- see
# docs/ARCHITECTURE.md for the full story). rmpc is an MPD client, not
# standalone -- mpd.service (systemd --user, shipped by the mpd package
# itself, `mpd/` package in this repo is just its mpd.conf) indexes and
# actually plays from ~/Music; rmpc is only ever the UI on top of it.
# Opens in its own floating kitty window rather than tiling -- a music
# player is something you glance at and dismiss, not something that
# should permanently claim a slot in the tiling layout the way an
# editor or browser does. Toggles via sway's scratchpad, not
# process-kill like the wofi-based popups above -- MPD keeps playing
# with no client attached at all (it's a real, independent daemon, more
# decoupled from its UI than termusic's own client/server split even
# was), so hiding the window should never stop playback. See
# rmpc-toggle.sh.
bindsym $mod+Shift+m exec ~/.local/bin/rmpc-toggle.sh

# Matches the app_id kitty is launched with above -- floating, sized to
# 60% width / 60% height of this machine's actual 1920x1080 output
# (1152x648 -- widened from an original 50% height so the window feels
# less cramped, matching the same 60% used for width instead of a
# smaller, mismatched fraction), centered, and moved into the
# scratchpad + shown immediately so it's visible the moment it first
# opens rather than launching hidden.
#
# blur enable: real compositor-level background blur (SwayFX,
# scenefx0.4 -- already in packages/aur.txt), the actual mechanism that
# makes this read as glass rather than just a tinted, see-through box --
# same lesson wofi's own layer_effects rule already established (see
# above), just reached via `for_window` instead: wofi is a layer-shell
# surface so it needed `layer_effects`, but rmpc is a plain toplevel
# window (kitty), and blur/corner_radius/shadows are all regular
# for_window-settable properties for those, no layer_effects block
# needed. corner_radius/shadows are NOT set here since this window
# already inherits the global corner_radius 12 / shadows on defaults
# (set above) the same as every other ordinary window -- only wofi
# needed its own explicit values, since layer-shell surfaces are exempt
# from those per-window toplevel defaults.
#
# Deliberately did NOT set blur_xray here, unlike wofi's rule -- SwayFX's
# own README says so explicitly for floating toplevel windows ("You
# probably want to set this to disable :)"), since blur_xray is meant
# for layer-shell panels that need to blur the true desktop background
# specifically rather than whatever's immediately behind them; a
# regular floating window blurring what's actually behind it (the
# wallpaper, since this one is centered above everything else) is
# already the right result without it.
for_window [app_id="^rmpc$"] floating enable, resize set 1152 648, move position center, move scratchpad, scratchpad show, blur enable

# mpv's video window (music-search.py's play action, audio-only mode
# never creates a window at all) -- floating and centered rather than
# tiling into whatever workspace happened to be focused, same
# "utility popup, not a real workspace citizen" treatment rmpc gets
# above. No blur/scratchpad here unlike rmpc -- this is meant to be
# watched immediately, not tucked away and toggled back into view.
# app_id confirmed directly (`swaymsg -t get_tree` while a real video
# was playing), not assumed from mpv's binary name.
#
# `focus` matters, not cosmetic: confirmed live that a window spawned
# this way (detached Popen from music-search.py, not from an already-
# focused terminal's own exec chain) does NOT get keyboard focus
# automatically in sway -- space/arrows/etc. silently went to whatever
# window was focused before, not to mpv, which is exactly "I can't
# control it" from the outside. Explicitly focusing here fixed it,
# verified via mpv's own IPC (`pause` property actually flipped after
# a spacebar press once focus was forced) before trusting it.
for_window [app_id="^mpv$"] floating enable, resize set 1280 720, move position center, focus

# Two direct keybindings instead of one + a mode toggle -- simpler to
# hold in your head than "press Y, then remember which mode is active".
# Both replace termusic's own `s` popup, which turned out to depend
# entirely on the public Invidious instance network (confirmed dead
# across the board, see docs/ARCHITECTURE.md and music-search.py's own
# docstring for the full story). wofi-based, so both route through
# toggle-popup.sh like every other picker above.

# Search YouTube, play the picked result as real video (mpv, a genuine
# controllable window -- see the for_window rule above) -- `video` is
# a required positional argument to music-search.py now, not a
# persisted toggle read behind the scenes.
bindsym $mod+Shift+y exec ~/.local/bin/toggle-popup.sh musicsearchvideo wofi ~/.local/bin/music-search.py video

# Search YouTube, add the picked result straight into MPD's queue via
# rmpc's own built-in `addyt` (rmpc already ships this -- yt-dlp under
# the hood, cached to ~/.cache/rmpc/youtube/, no reason to hand-roll a
# second download path) and open rmpc so it's immediately visible and
# controllable with every normal rmpc/MPD control (play/pause/next/
# prev/seek/volume) -- this is "opens in the music player", not a
# headless process with no way to stop it.
#
# Needs python-mutagen installed (`sudo pacman -S python-mutagen`) --
# confirmed live that addyt's own post-processing step fails without
# it (rmpc debuginfo already flags this as "not installed" on this
# machine) and surfaces a clear error notification if it's still
# missing, rather than failing silently.
bindsym $mod+Ctrl+Shift+y exec ~/.local/bin/toggle-popup.sh musicsearchmusic wofi ~/.local/bin/music-search.py music

# Cycle notification mode (normal -> silent -> dnd -> normal) -- same
# script and same three-state cycle the waybar bell's scroll wheel uses
# (on-scroll-up/-down in waybar/config), just the keyboard path. Only
# one direction bound here since scroll already covers both, and
# cycling forward through 3 states by pressing repeatedly is simple
# enough not to need a second reverse-cycle keybinding to remember too.
bindsym $mod+n exec ~/.local/bin/notification-mode.sh next

# Direct jumps to a specific notification mode, alongside the cycle
# above -- for "I know exactly which mode I want right now" (DND before
# a meeting, say) rather than pressing $mod+n repeatedly and counting
# states.
bindsym $mod+Ctrl+n exec ~/.local/bin/notification-mode.sh normal
bindsym $mod+Ctrl+s exec ~/.local/bin/notification-mode.sh silent
bindsym $mod+Ctrl+d exec ~/.local/bin/notification-mode.sh dnd

# $mod+? matches the near-universal "show shortcuts" convention (Slack,
# Discord, GitHub, GNOME all use ? or Ctrl+/) rather than inventing a new
# one. Excludes Neovim on purpose -- it already has its own keymap search.
# Search all keybindings (sway + tmux) in a fuzzy wofi popup
bindsym $mod+Shift+slash exec ~/.local/bin/toggle-popup.sh keybindsearch wofi ~/.local/bin/keybind-search.py

### Window Look & Feel (Classy Minimalist)
# Core border style for windows
default_border pixel 2
default_floating_border pixel 2
font pango:JetBrainsMono Nerd Font 12

# --- Window Geometry (Margins / Gaps) ---
# Inner gaps separate adjacent windows from each other
gaps inner 4
# Outer gaps separate windows from the screen edges and Waybar
gaps outer 4
# inverse_outer (not plain "on"): outer (screen-edge) gaps show
# specifically when a workspace has exactly one window -- the common
# case -- so there's always a visible margin to the screen edge then.
# With multiple tiled windows, outer gaps hide (inner gaps between the
# windows still apply) so tiling doesn't waste edge space. Free either
# way: same gap calculation sway already does every frame.
smart_gaps inverse_outer

# Tried workspace_auto_back_and_forth (pressing the key for the workspace
# you're already on jumps back to whichever one you were on before, like
# alt-tab) -- reverted: pressing $mod+1 while already on workspace 1
# should just stay there, not silently toggle away to wherever you'd
# been previously. Sway's default (off) is what's wanted here.

# --- SwayFX Aesthetics (Rounded Corners & Shadows) ---
# Smooth corners that match the curvature of your Waybar pills
corner_radius 12
# Subtle drop-shadows to give windows separation from the wallpaper
shadows on
shadow_blur_radius 20
shadow_color #0000007F

# Real compositor-level background blur for wofi specifically, via
# SwayFX's layer_effects (scenefx0.4 -- already in packages/aur.txt,
# nothing new to install). This is what makes "glass" actually read as
# glass instead of just a tinted box: wofi/style.css's own semi-transparent
# background alone has nothing real to blur behind it without this.
# Namespace confirmed live rather than guessed -- `swaymsg -t get_outputs`
# while wofi was open lists it under layer_shell_surfaces as "wofi" (not
# assumed from the binary name; layer-shell namespaces don't have to match
# it). blur_xray blurs the true desktop background behind wofi rather than
# whatever surface happens to be immediately behind it (e.g. a waybar
# pill it overlaps), which is the difference between an authentic frosted-
# glass look and blurring the wrong thing. corner_radius matches wofi's
# own CSS border-radius so the blurred/shadowed shape lines up with the
# window's actual rounded corners instead of showing a square blur behind
# round content.
layer_effects "wofi" {
    blur enable
    blur_xray enable
    corner_radius 16
    shadows enable
}

# --- Theme Window Borders ---
# Color configuration for window frames
# Focused border: Red. Went Mauve -> Sapphire -> Lavender -> Mauve before
# this -- asked for something more "dominant" than Mauve read as. Red is
# the single boldest, most commanding hue in this palette -- everywhere
# else in this setup it's reserved for real alerts (network disconnected,
# battery critical, the power button), which is a real trade-off worth
# naming: unlike those, this is a *persistent* fixture on every focused
# window, not a transient alert, and the two read differently enough in
# practice (a thin border outline vs. a whole waybar pill going red) that
# it shouldn't actually get mistaken for "something's wrong" -- but flag
# it if it ever does read that way.
# class                 border  bground text    indicator child_border
client.focused          #F38BA8 #11111B #CDD6F4 #F38BA8   #F38BA8
client.focused_inactive #45475A #1E1E2E #A6ADC8 #45475A   #45475A
client.unfocused        #313244 #1E1E2E #6C7086 #313244   #313244


### Wallpaper configuration
# Daily rotation is handled by systemd/.config/systemd/user/wallpaper.timer
# (fetch_wallpaper.sh) -- once a day, not on every reload. This just sets
# the background at sway startup/reload from whatever current.jpg already is.
output * bg /home/yash/Pictures/Wallpapers/current.jpg fill

### Input configuration
input type:touchpad {
	tap enabled
		natural_scroll enabled
}

# No cursor theme was ever explicitly set anywhere (checked: not in env,
# not here, not in gtk-3.0/settings.ini) -- apps were left to each fall
# back to their own default resolution, inconsistently. "default" already
# resolves to Adwaita (/usr/share/icons/default/index.theme), just never
# told to anything explicitly. Real, worthwhile fix on its own regardless
# of any one app's cursor behavior.
seat seat0 xcursor_theme Adwaita 24

### Key bindings
#
# Basics:
#
# Start a terminal
bindsym $mod+Return exec $term

# Start your combined app/command launcher
bindsym $mod+d exec ~/.local/bin/toggle-popup.sh launcher wofi $menu

# Start your math/currency calculator
bindsym $mod+c exec ~/.local/bin/toggle-popup.sh calc wofi $calc

# Kill focused window
bindsym $mod+Shift+q kill

# Drag floating windows by holding down $mod and left mouse button.
# Resize them with right mouse button + $mod.
floating_modifier $mod normal

# Reload the configuration file
bindsym $mod+Shift+c reload

# Exit sway (logs you out of your Wayland session)
bindsym $mod+Shift+e exec swaynag -t warning -m 'You pressed the exit shortcut. Do you really want to exit sway? This will end your Wayland session.' -B 'Yes, exit sway' 'swaymsg exit'

#
# Moving around:
#
# Move your focus around
bindsym $mod+$left focus left
bindsym $mod+$down focus down
bindsym $mod+$up focus up
bindsym $mod+$right focus right
# Or use $mod+[up|down|left|right]
bindsym $mod+Left focus left
bindsym $mod+Down focus down
bindsym $mod+Up focus up
bindsym $mod+Right focus right

# Move the focused window with the same, but add Shift
bindsym $mod+Shift+$left move left
bindsym $mod+Shift+$down move down
bindsym $mod+Shift+$up move up
bindsym $mod+Shift+$right move right
# Ditto, with arrow keys
bindsym $mod+Shift+Left move left
bindsym $mod+Shift+Down move down
bindsym $mod+Shift+Up move up
bindsym $mod+Shift+Right move right

#
